  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Kansas City walked away 6-2. The Athletics never recovered from a second inning that got away early, and Gage Jump lasted only four innings. Eight hits and six strikeouts is not the profile of a team that competes. It's the sound of swinging through a game you've already lost.",
    "what_this_means": "They stranded runners at will. Twelve RISP situations produced just two runs, leaving critical moments in the second and fourth innings empty. Jump gave up five runs before the halfway point. Taylor Rashi and Scott Blewett kept the bleeding contained after that, combining for four scoreless innings, but there was nothing left to work with. Lawrence Butler and Carlos Cortes each went 2-for-3 as individual efforts, but a second inning with runners in scoring position and nothing to show for it defined how this game felt. Controlled chaos that favored Kansas City. By the time the Athletics scored two in the fifth, the game was already decided. This is what inconsistency looks like in real time: enough contact to be dangerous, enough failure to be fatal.",
    "what_to_watch": "Hayden Wesneski takes the mound for Houston tonight, carrying a 5.16 ERA into a matchup where J.T. Ginn has looked sharp lately. 2.99 over his last four starts.",
